mparison is useful: one side tests grounding by constructing troublesome truthmaking chains, and the other asks whether grounding can be analyzed through identity-like structure that is not hostage to fact-shaped relata. The strongest reading is not that one paper defeats the other, but that they expose different costs of grounding theory.\\\",\n            \\\"parent_ref\\\": \\\"draft-4\\\",\n            \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n                \\\"score\\\": 24,\n                \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Synthesizes both sources while avoiding an unsupported claim of direct refutation.\\\"\n            }\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\nwith output_path.open('w', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    json.dump(payload, f, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)\n    f.write('\\\\n')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
